{{Further|LGBTQ representations in hip hop music}}
The song and music video received praise from several outlets for being "unabashedly queer".<ref name="unabashedly" /><ref name="changed">{{Cite web|url=https://variety.com/2021/music/news/lil-nas-x-montero-queer-music-artists-sexuality-1234939238/|title=Lil Nas X's Sexed-Up 'Montero' Video Has Changed Everything for Queer Music Artists|date=March 26, 2021|first=Adam B.|last=Vary|work=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]|access-date=March 27, 2021|archive-date=March 27, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210327003627/https://variety.com/2021/music/news/lil-nas-x-montero-queer-music-artists-sexuality-1234939238/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.wmagazine.com/culture/lil-nas-x-montero-music-video|title=Lil Nas X's "Montero" Is an Encouraging Note to Self|date=March 26, 2021|first=Brooke|last=Marine|work=[[W (magazine)|W]]|access-date=March 27, 2021|archive-date=March 29, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210329131509/https://www.wmagazine.com/culture/lil-nas-x-montero-music-video|url-status=live}}</ref> ''[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]''{{'s}} Adam B. Vary wrote that the video "changed everything for queer music artists", noting that some LGBT artists like [[Jonathan Knight]] of [[New Kids on the Block]] and [[Lance Bass]] of [[NSYNC]] sang about women while staying [[closeted]], while others like [[Elton John]] and [[Ricky Martin]] did not explicitly sing about their sexuality.<ref name="changed" /> He described the sexual imagery, including the pole dance, as evoking images of Madonna or [[Janet Jackson]], without the need to hide his [[homosexuality]].<ref name="changed" /> He also remarked that artists like [[Frank Ocean]] and [[Troye Sivan]] were bolder about their sexuality, but had never reached the same level of commercial success as Lil Nas X.<ref name="changed" /> David Harris, a magister of the [[Church of Satan]], approved of the music video's portrayal of [[Sexual consent|consensual sexuality]] as well as the ending of Lil Nas X crowning himself Satan.<ref name="satanists"/>

The music video received criticism from conservative commentators in the United States who deemed it immoral or harmful to children.<ref name="satanists">{{Cite magazine|url=https://www.rollingstone.com/culture/culture-news/lil-nas-x-montero-call-me-by-your-name-video-church-of-satan-1147634/|title=We Asked Satanists What They Think of the New Lil Nas X Video|date=March 26, 2021|first=EJ|last=Dickson|magazine=[[Rolling Stone]]|access-date=March 27, 2021|archive-date=March 27, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210327001759/https://www.rollingstone.com/culture/culture-news/lil-nas-x-montero-call-me-by-your-name-video-church-of-satan-1147634/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="shuts">{{Cite web|url=https://www.independent.co.uk/arts-entertainment/music/news/lil-nas-x-satan-call-me-by-your-name-video-b1823325.html|title=Lil Nas X shuts down right-wing criticism of Call Me By Your Name video|date=March 27, 2021|first=Roisin|last=O'Connor|work=[[The Independent]]|access-date=March 27, 2021|archive-date=March 27, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210327120639/https://www.independent.co.uk/arts-entertainment/music/news/lil-nas-x-satan-call-me-by-your-name-video-b1823325.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="responds">{{Cite web|url=https://www.complex.com/music/lil-nas-x-montero-video-backlash-kids/|title=Lil Nas X Responds to Backlash Over Provocative "Montero" Video: 'I Am An Adult'|date=March 27, 2021|first=Joshua|last=Espinoza|work=[[Complex (magazine)|Complex]]|access-date=March 27, 2021|archive-date=March 27, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210327212553/https://www.complex.com/music/lil-nas-x-montero-video-backlash-kids/|url-status=live}}</ref> [[Governor of South Dakota]] [[Kristi Noem]],<ref name="today">
{{Cite web|url=https://www.today.com/popculture/lil-nas-x-sparks-outrage-new-music-video-sneakers-t213219|title=Lil Nas X sparks outrage with new music video and sneakers, responds to critics|date=March 28, 2021|first=Francesca|last=Gariano|work=[[Today (American TV program)|Today]]|access-date=March 29, 2021|archive-date=March 29, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210329131600/https://www.today.com/popculture/lil-nas-x-sparks-outrage-new-music-video-sneakers-t213219|url-status=live}}
</ref> conservative pundit [[Candace Owens]],<ref name="today"/> [[Kaitlin Bennett]],<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.newsweek.com/lil-nas-x-kaitlin-bennett-feud-explained-twitter-montero-call-me-your-name-1579400|title=Lil Nas X, Kaitlin Bennett Feud Explained as Two Trade Blows on Twitter|website=Newsweek|date=March 29, 2021}}</ref> and evangelical pastor [[Mark Burns (pastor)|Mark Burns]]<ref name="cnn-shoes">
{{Cite web|url=https://edition.cnn.com/style/article/lil-nas-x-mschf-satan-nike-shoes/index.html|title=Lil Nas X releases unofficial 'Satan' Nikes containing human blood|date=March 29, 2021|first=Oscar|last=Holland|publisher=[[CNN]]|access-date=March 29, 2021|archive-date=March 29, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210329061850/https://edition.cnn.com/style/article/lil-nas-x-mschf-satan-nike-shoes/index.html|url-status=live}}
</ref> all reacted negatively on Twitter, as did rapper [[Joyner Lucas]]<ref>
{{cite web|url=https://www.newsweek.com/lil-nas-x-shuts-down-joyner-lucas-montero-criticism-1579436|title=Lil Nas X Shuts Down Joyner Lucas Over 'Montero' Criticism|last=Nolan|first=Emma|date=March 29, 2021|work=Newsweek}}
</ref> and athletes [[Nick Young (basketball)|Nick Young]]<ref name="today"/> and [[Trevor Lawrence]].<ref name="trevor">{{Cite web|url=https://247sports.com/Article/Trevor-Lawrence-admonishes-Satan-shoes-from-rapper-Lil-Nas-X-163284799/|title=Trevor Lawrence admonishes Satan shoes from rapper Lil Nas X|date=March 29, 2021|first=Brad|last=Crawford|work=[[247Sports]]|access-date=March 30, 2021|archive-date=March 29, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210329143930/https://247sports.com/Article/Trevor-Lawrence-admonishes-Satan-shoes-from-rapper-Lil-Nas-X-163284799/|url-status=live}}</ref> In response to criticism, Lil Nas X wrote on Twitter: "There is a [[Mass shootings in the United States|mass shooting every week]] that [[Federal government of the United States|our government]] does nothing to stop. Me sliding down a [[CGI Animation|CGI]] pole isn't what's destroying society" and "I am an adult. I am not gonna spend my entire career trying to cater to your children. That is your job", among other retorts.<ref name="shuts" /><ref name="responds" /> However, he also confessed that the backlash was "putting an emotional toll" on him.<ref name="CBS News" />

The negative reception to the song and music video was characterized by ''[[Los Angeles Times]]'' and ''[[Vice (magazine)|Vice]]'' as illustrating a [[Satanic panic]] and compared to past moments in popular music history, including [[jazz music]] being referred to as "the devil's music" in the early [[20th century music|20th century]], [[John Lennon]]'s comment in a 1966 interview that [[the Beatles]] were "[[more popular than Jesus]]", [[backmasking]] accusations, the [[Parents Music Resource Center]]'s "Filthy Fifteen", Madonna's music video for "[[Like a Prayer (song)|Like a Prayer]]", [[Lady Gaga]]'s music videos for "[[Judas (Lady Gaga song)|Judas]]" and "[[Alejandro (song)|Alejandro]]", and [[Nicki Minaj]]'s performance of "[[Roman Holiday (song)|Roman Holiday]]" at the [[54th Annual Grammy Awards]].<ref name="vicesatanic">{{Cite web|last=Terry|first=Josh|date=March 29, 2021|title=Lil Nas X Isn't the First Pop Star to Spark a Satanic Panic|url=https://www.vice.com/en/article/lil-nas-x-isnt-the-first-pop-star-to-spark-a-satanic-panic/|website=[[Vice (magazine)|Vice]]|access-date=March 30, 2021}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.latimes.com/entertainment-arts/music/story/2021-03-29/lil-nas-x-montero-satanic-panic|title=Lil Nas X's 'Montero' and the delight of yet another satanic panic|date=March 29, 2021|first=Mikael|last=Wood|work=[[Los Angeles Times]]|access-date=March 30, 2021}}</ref> ''[[Esquire (magazine)|Esquire]]'' referred to it as "beelzepop" (a play on [[Beelzebub]]) and similarly compared reception to Gaga's "Judas" and Minaj's "Roman Holiday".<ref>{{Cite web|last=Clark|first=Murray|date=March 29, 2021|title=Just How Evil Are Lil Nas X and MSCHF's Blood-Filled Nikes?|url=https://www.esquire.com/uk/style/a35963060/lil-nas-x-nike-mschf-satan-shoes/|work=[[Esquire (magazine)|Esquire]]|access-date=March 30, 2021}}</ref>

The controversy has been described as being part of the marketing strategy for the song. Neena Rouhani of ''[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]'' depicted the social media outrage as his "greatest marketing tool", and Kevin Meenan, a music charts manager at [[YouTube]], described it as "very explicit", and that controversy was his goal.<ref name="Billboard marketing">{{cite magazine |last=Rouhani |first=Neena |date=April 6, 2021 |magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]] |title='He's a Genius': How Lil Nas X's 'Montero' Marketing Strategy Pressed All the Right Buttons |url=https://www.billboard.com/pro/lil-nas-x-montero-marketing-analysis/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210406165030/https://www.billboard.com/articles/columns/pop/9551819/lil-nas-x-montero-marketing-analysis/ |archive-date=April 6, 2021 |url-status=live }}</ref> [[Jon Caramanica]] of ''[[The New York Times]]'' compared the controversy to bait, and a performance of [[moral panic]], noting how he uses Twitter as a "performance space ... with an almost limitless audience".<ref name="NYTimes controversy">{{cite web |last=Caramanica |first=Jon |date=March 30, 2021 |work=[[The New York Times]] |title=Lil Nas X, Clapback Champ |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/30/arts/music/lil-nas-x-twitter-sneakers-outrage.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210501132738/https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/30/arts/music/lil-nas-x-twitter-sneakers-outrage.html |archive-date=May 1, 2021 |url-status=live |url-access=limited }}</ref>

The visuals of the music video became popular on social media, with the pole dance becoming a [[internet meme|meme]] on [[TikTok]].<ref name="Insider TikTok">{{cite web |last=Haasch |first=Palmer |date=March 29, 2021 |work=[[Insider Inc.|Insider]] |title=Lil Nas X's 'Montero' pole dancing descent to hell has become a TikTok meme |url=https://www.insider.com/lil-nas-x-montero-video-tiktok-meme-pole-dancing-hell-2021-3 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20210505125526/https://www.insider.com/lil-nas-x-montero-video-tiktok-meme-pole-dancing-hell-2021-3 |archive-date=May 5, 2021 |url-status=live }}</ref> Additionally, the music video and its reception were lampooned on a ''Saturday Night Live'' [[cold open]], with [[Chris Redd]] as Lil Nas X discussing the controversy with [[Chloe Fineman]] as Britney Spears.<ref>{{Cite magazine|url=https://www.rollingstone.com/tv/tv-news/snl-lil-nas-x-matt-gaetz-britney-spears-talk-show-1150900/|title='SNL': Lil Nas X, Matt Gaetz Address Controversies on Britney Spears Talk Show|date=April 4, 2021|first=Daniel|last=Kreps|magazine=[[Rolling Stone]]|access-date=May 6, 2021}}</ref> In June 2021, a {{convert|36|x|36|ft|m|abbr=on}} mural depicting three of Lil Nas X's looks in the music video was put up in [[Gayborhood, Philadelphia|Philadelphia's Gayborhood]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.phillymag.com/news/2021/06/03/lil-nas-x-mural-philadelphia|title=Check Out the Gayborhood's New 36-Foot-Tall Lil Nas X Mural|date=June 3, 2021|first=Victor|last=Fiorillo|work=[[Philadelphia (magazine)|Philadelphia]]|access-date=June 28, 2021}}</ref>

